<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">We study the problem of sampling from
<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(\mathrm{d}x)\propto e^{-V(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(\mathrm{d}x)\propto e^{-V(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-strongly convex and <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth, and write
<math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\coloneqq\beta/\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\coloneqq\beta/\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. We design and analyze the Proximal Bouncy
Particle Sampler (Proximal BPS), a new sampler that combines ideas from the proximal sampler and the bouncy particle sampler.
From a warm start initialization with <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> Rényi divergence
w.r.t. <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, Proximal BPS returns a sample whose law is <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-close to <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> in total variation distance using
<math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\sqrt{\kappa}\,d^{1/4}\,\mathrm{polylog}(1/\varepsilon))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>polylog</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\sqrt{\kappa}\,d^{1/4}\,\mathrm{polylog}(1/\varepsilon))</annotation></semantics></math> gradient queries in expectation.</span></p>
  
</div>
