</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.4.24" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.4.24.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.4.24.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:48.3pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.4.24.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.T1.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.4.24.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.4.24.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:107.0pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.4.24.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S2.T1.4.24.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Result-specific constants, fixed where introduced.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.4.24.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.4.24.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:48.3pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.4.24.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.T1.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.800em">O</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">B</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(B)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.4.24.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.75pt;padding-bottom:0.75pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.4.24.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:107.0pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.4.24.4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><math id="S2.T1.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.4.24.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> times a fixed power of logarithms in the
problem parameters.</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Frequently used notation, grouped by theme (continued).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Overview of the construction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first describe the Proximal BPS at a high level. <a href="#S3.F1" title="In 3.1 Overview of the construction ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> gives a geometric overview.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2609.06905v1/figure-algorithm.svg" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:390/316;" width="390" height="316"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Geometric overview of Proximal BPS.
(a) The initial law is lifted to the augmented distribution. At each
iteration, (b) the auxiliary point is reflected through the current
position, and (c) a conditional reference point is drawn. With
probability <math id="S3.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, that point becomes the next position; with
probability <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, it determines the equilibrium position
<math id="S3.F1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c=Y^{+}-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=Y^{+}-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{X})</annotation></semantics></math> of the deterministic harmonic flow
for the time-<math id="S3.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> BPS half-turn process in (d), which starts from the current
position.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baseline Gibbs update and reflection of the auxiliary variable.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The usual proximal sampler for the augmentation
(<a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 2.6 ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>) is the two-block Gibbs update</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{+}\sim\mathcal{N}(x,\eta I)\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX^{+}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=Y^{+}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{+}\sim\mathcal{N}(x,\eta I)\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX^{+}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=Y^{+}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Each update discards the previous value of the coordinate it changes:
the first adds fresh Gaussian noise to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, while the second makes an
independent conditional draw using a restricted Gaussian oracle (RGO).
The latter conditional law is local and well-conditioned by
(<a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 2.10 ‣ Item (i) ‣ Proposition 2.1 (Geometry of the joint law). ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>), which makes the RGO inexpensive for
small <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. The same localization, however, generally requires more
iterations, so <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> balances the cost of each conditional
update against the number of iterations. This construction and its RGO
implementation are studied in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Proximal BPS preserves the same augmented target but replaces this
two-block Gibbs transition with one that retains dependence on the
preceding augmented state. This memory plays the role of momentum and
underlies the kinetic acceleration established below. For the
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>-update, the fresh Gaussian draw is replaced by the deterministic
reflection (see Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Overview of the construction ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b))</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{+}=2x-y\,,\hskip 20.00003pty^{+}-x=-(y-x)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{+}=2x-y\,,\hskip 20.00003pty^{+}-x=-(y-x)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This map preserves <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, because it negates the centered Gaussian
residual <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y-X\mid X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y-X\mid X</annotation></semantics></math> without changing its conditional law. Consequently, the residual from the current augmented
state is carried into the next position update rather than discarded and
replaced by fresh Gaussian noise. The <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>-update described next also
retains dependence on the current position along the dominant branch,
which runs the half-turn process; only occasional conditional resampling
discards this memory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Conditional reference point and occasional resampling.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After the reflection sets <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=y^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=y^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, Proximal BPS updates <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> using a
mixture of two kernels that preserve
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math>: independent conditional resampling and the
kernel induced by the conditional half-turn process defined below. Draw</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX^{+}=\begin{cases}\widetilde{X}\,,&amp;\text{with probability }\rho\quad\text{(conditional resampling)}\,,\\
x_{\pi}\,,&amp;\text{with probability }1-\rho\quad\text{(half-turn process, defined below)}\,.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.150em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>with probability </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(conditional resampling)</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.150em">,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>with probability </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(half-turn process, defined below)</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptX^{+}=\begin{cases}\widetilde{X}\,,&amp;\text{with probability }\rho\quad\text{(conditional resampling)}\,,\\
x_{\pi}\,,&amp;\text{with probability }1-\rho\quad\text{(half-turn process, defined below)}\,.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Choosing <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>
small makes the half-turn process the dominant option,
while conditional resampling prevents periodicity.
When conditional resampling is selected, the RGO output <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math>
is the next position. On the dominant branch, the process uses
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math> as an auxiliary reference point, and its output <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>
remains dependent on the current position.
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (𝜒^2 convergence). ‣ 3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> shows that composing this update with the
reflection <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{+}=2x-y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{+}=2x-y</annotation></semantics></math> accelerates convergence and thus reduces the
iteration complexity, as the
updates carry information across iterations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The conditional half-turn process.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This process updates <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> through a kernel that preserves
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}</annotation></semantics></math> while retaining dependence on the current
position, rather than replacing <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by an independent conditional
draw. For this purpose, we construct a stochastic process on phase
space <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,p)</annotation></semantics></math> with invariant law
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{+}}\otimes\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>. Each run starts from
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}=x</annotation></semantics></math> and an independent <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>. This momentum is
temporary: it is discarded at the end of the process, whereas the memory
across iterations remains in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At fixed <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=y^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=y^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, vanilla BPS would use straight-line motion and bounce against the full gradient of
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="V(\cdot)+\|{\cdot}-y^{+}\|^{2}/(2\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\cdot)+\|{\cdot}-y^{+}\|^{2}/(2\eta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Although exact, this does not exploit the fact that we
know the quadratic coupling potential exactly, which dominates <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is
small. We thus incorporate this quadratic term into the deterministic
motion between bounce events, which account for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A direct version of this idea would still bounce against the full
gradient <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, whose magnitude need not be small even inside
the conditional region localized near <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is where the conditional reference point <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is used.
The fixed gradient <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V(\widetilde{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V(\widetilde{X})</annotation></semantics></math> is incorporated into the
deterministic motion, whose equilibrium position is
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c\coloneqq y^{+}-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>≔</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\coloneqq y^{+}-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{X})</annotation></semantics></math>. Bounces handle only
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})</annotation></semantics></math>, which makes the bounce rate small
due to smoothness of the potential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Ignoring bounces, the harmonic motion traces a periodic orbit around
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and returns to its initial point after time <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. By a
<em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">half-turn</em> we mean stopping halfway around this orbit, at time
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. See Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.1 Overview of the construction ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(d). This midpoint is chosen to preserve memory. The temporary
momentum is needed to define invariant phase space dynamics, but it
should not replace the information carried by the current position.
If we stop after a quarter-period, the new position
would be determined by the freshly drawn momentum rather than by
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Continuing through a full period would undo the move and
return to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The half-period is the first non-trivial stopping time
that avoids both failures: the fresh momentum no longer contributes to
the position, and the displacement from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is retained with its
direction reversed. Thus the no-bounce map is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{\pi},p_{\pi})=(2c-x_{0},-p_{0})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{\pi},p_{\pi})=(2c-x_{0},-p_{0})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The BPS bounces correct for <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> along the trajectory, but the process
keeps this time-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> horizon so that its dominant harmonic motion
retains the same memory-preserving geometry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The rest of this section formalizes the construction. We reserve
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,\pi]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,\pi]</annotation></semantics></math> for the phase space process <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},p_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},p_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> during one run
of the half-turn process
and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for the Markov chain <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{k},Y_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{k},Y_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> constructs the conditional half-turn process
and proves reversibility of its induced kernel;
<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> defines the full ideal
transition. The practical gradient-only
implementation is discussed in <a href="#S4" title="4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>The ideal conditional half-turn process</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=y</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall from (<a href="#S2.E8" title="Equation 2.8 ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>) that the position
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to be sampled has conditional law</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x)\propto e^{-V_{y}(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x\,,\hskip 20.00003ptV_{y}(x)\coloneqq V(x)+\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x)\propto e^{-V_{y}(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x\,,\hskip 20.00003ptV_{y}(x)\coloneqq V(x)+\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We construct the half-turn process so that its induced transition
preserves this law. The
construction starts from vanilla BPS for the conditional target, absorbs
the quadratic coupling potential into the deterministic motion, and then
introduces
an independent conditional reference point to localize the remaining
bounce rate. We begin by introducing a Gaussian momentum
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>. The phase space law to be preserved is</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\eta,y}(\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}p)\coloneqq\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x)\,\mathcal{N}(0,I)(\mathrm{d}p)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V_{y}(x)-\frac{\|p\|^{2}}{2}\Bigr\}\,\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}p\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\eta,y}(\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}p)\coloneqq\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}(\mathrm{d}x)\,\mathcal{N}(0,I)(\mathrm{d}p)\propto\exp\Bigl\{-V_{y}(x)-\frac{\|p\|^{2}}{2}\Bigr\}\,\mathrm{d}x\,\mathrm{d}p\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Recall from (<a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 2.4 ‣ 2.1 The standard Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>) that <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes specular
reflection across the hyperplane orthogonal to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A natural first attempt is to apply vanilla BPS directly to this
conditional distribution. Its infinitesimal generator acts on a smooth
test function <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}(\mathsf{L}_{y}^{\rm vanilla}f)(x,p)&amp;=\sqrt{\eta}\,p^{\top}\nabla_{x}f(x,p)\\
&amp;\quad+\sqrt{\eta}\,[p^{\top}\nabla V_{y}(x)]_{+}\bigl\{f(x,R_{\nabla V_{y}(x)}p)-f(x,p)\bigr\}\,.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>vanilla</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.300em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.300em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.022em">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}(\mathsf{L}_{y}^{\rm vanilla}f)(x,p)&amp;=\sqrt{\eta}\,p^{\top}\nabla_{x}f(x,p)\\
&amp;\quad+\sqrt{\eta}\,[p^{\top}\nabla V_{y}(x)]_{+}\bigl\{f(x,R_{\nabla V_{y}(x)}p)-f(x,p)\bigr\}\,.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The common factor <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> only fixes the unit of time; it does not
change the invariant law. We choose this unit so that the harmonic motion
introduced below has period <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.
The first term gives straight-line motion, while the second reflects the
momentum against the full conditional gradient. This process is exact,
but it can be expensive: even when <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the quadratic coupling potential
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|{\cdot}-y\|^{2}/(2\eta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\cdot}-y\|^{2}/(2\eta)</annotation></semantics></math> produces a stationary mean bounce rate of order
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To reduce the number of bounce events, we put the quadratic part of the
conditional gradient into the deterministic dynamics. This gives the
generator</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}(\mathsf{L}_{y}^{\rm harm}f)(x,p)&amp;=\sqrt{\eta}\,p^{\top}\nabla_{x}f(x,p)-\frac{(x-y)^{\top}}{\sqrt{\eta}}\nabla_{p}f(x,p)\\
&amp;\quad+\sqrt{\eta}\,[p^{\top}\nabla V(x)]_{+}\bigl\{f(x,R_{\nabla V(x)}p)-f(x,p)\bigr\}\,.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖫</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>harm</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.300em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.300em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.022em">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}(\mathsf{L}_{y}^{\rm harm}f)(x,p)&amp;=\sqrt{\eta}\,p^{\top}\nabla_{x}f(x,p)-\frac{(x-y)^{\top}}{\sqrt{\eta}}\nabla_{p}f(x,p)\\
&amp;\quad+\sqrt{\eta}\,[p^{\top}\nabla V(x)]_{+}\bigl\{f(x,R_{\nabla V(x)}p)-f(x,p)\bigr\}\,.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The first line is the Hamiltonian flow associated with the quadratic
coupling potential: between bounces, it rotates <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x-y)/\sqrt{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x-y)/\sqrt{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. This use of a quadratic reference flow, with the remaining force
handled by bounce events, is analogous to the Boomerang
sampler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
The direct harmonic generator (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3.3 ‣ 3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>)
still bounces against the full gradient <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V</annotation></semantics></math>, so its bounce rate
can remain large.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To further reduce the bounce rate and the resulting computational
cost, we introduce a conditional reference point and use the smoothness of
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> to control the local gradient difference. Specifically, draw
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}</annotation></semantics></math> independently and write the exact
decomposition</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V(x)=\nabla V(\widetilde{X})+\{\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})\}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V(x)=\nabla V(\widetilde{X})+\{\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})\}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Conditionally on <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}=\widetilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}=\widetilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, we incorporate the fixed
reference term into the deterministic dynamics and retain only the gradient
difference in the BPS jump term. Define the equilibrium position of this
deterministic harmonic flow and the residual gradient by</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{y,\widetilde{x}}\coloneqq y-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{x})\,,\hskip 20.00003pth_{\widetilde{x}}(x)\coloneqq\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{x})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{y,\widetilde{x}}\coloneqq y-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{x})\,,\hskip 20.00003pth_{\widetilde{x}}(x)\coloneqq\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{x})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The resulting generator is</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{x}}f(x,p)&amp;\coloneqq\sqrt{\eta}\,p^{\top}\nabla_{x}f(x,p)-\frac{(x-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})^{\top}}{\sqrt{\eta}}\nabla_{p}f(x,p)\\
&amp;\quad+\sqrt{\eta}\,[p^{\top}h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)]_{+}\bigl\{f(x,R_{h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)}p)-f(x,p)\bigr\}\,.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>𝖫</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.300em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>p</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.300em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.800em">~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" rspace="0.022em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathsf{L}_{y,\widetilde{x}}f(x,p)&amp;\coloneqq\sqrt{\eta}\,p^{\top}\nabla_{x}f(x,p)-\frac{(x-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})^{\top}}{\sqrt{\eta}}\nabla_{p}f(x,p)\\
&amp;\quad+\sqrt{\eta}\,[p^{\top}h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)]_{+}\bigl\{f(x,R_{h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)}p)-f(x,p)\bigr\}\,.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.4" class="ltx_p">The harmonic and residual terms sum to the full conditional force
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sqrt{\eta}\,\nabla V_{y}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.317em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\eta}\,\nabla V_{y}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math> changes only how this
force is divided between deterministic motion and bounces. By the
<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-smoothness of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)\|=\|\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{x})\|\leq\beta\,\|x-\widetilde{x}\|\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|h_{\widetilde{x}}(x)\|=\|\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{x})\|\leq\beta\,\|x-\widetilde{x}\|\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.5" class="ltx_p">Drawing the reference point from the same localized conditional law keeps
the residual gradient small and reduces the expected number of bounces per
run of the half-turn process to order <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\eta\sqrt{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\eta\sqrt{d}</annotation></semantics></math>; see
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3 (Stationary bounce count). ‣ 3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Having specified the dynamics, it remains to choose the integration
horizon. For fixed <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y,\widetilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y,\widetilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, the deterministic part of
(<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 3.5 ‣ 3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>) is harmonic motion about its equilibrium
position <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{y,\widetilde{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{y,\widetilde{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the absence of bounces, its position at
time <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}}=(x_{0}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})\cos t+\sqrt{\eta}\,p_{0}\sin t\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}}=(x_{0}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})\cos t+\sqrt{\eta}\,p_{0}\sin t\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">As discussed earlier, we choose the integration horizon <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to one
half-period. This is the first non-zero harmonic time at which the
position <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> no longer depends on the initial momentum <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Without bounces, the resulting map is</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{\pi},p_{\pi})=(2c_{y,\widetilde{x}}-x_{0},-p_{0})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{\pi},p_{\pi})=(2c_{y,\widetilde{x}}-x_{0},-p_{0})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, the position is reflected through the equilibrium position
<math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{y,\widetilde{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{y,\widetilde{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and retains its dependence on <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Bounce
events correct for the residual force along the trajectory, while the
fixed horizon <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\pi</annotation></semantics></math> retains the memory-preserving structure of the
half-turn process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The construction is now complete. The following algorithm gives its
pathwise prescription: it draws the reference point and momentum, evolves
the process with generator (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 3.5 ‣ 3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>) for time <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, and
returns only the final position.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Simulation of the ideal conditional half-turn process</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> state <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Draw <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> independently.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            Query <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V(\widetilde{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V(\widetilde{X})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            Set <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\leftarrow x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\leftarrow x</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            Evolve <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},p_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},p_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> according to

<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\dot{x}=\sqrt{\eta}\,p\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\dot{p}=-\frac{x-y}{\sqrt{\eta}}-\sqrt{\eta}\,\nabla V(\widetilde{X})\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">˙</mo></mover><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.317em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dot{x}=\sqrt{\eta}\,p\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\dot{p}=-\frac{x-y}{\sqrt{\eta}}-\sqrt{\eta}\,\nabla V(\widetilde{X})\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>until <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            During the evolution, generate bounce events at rate

<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\widetilde{X}}(x,p)=\sqrt{\eta}\,\bigl[p^{\top}[\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})]\bigr]_{+}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\widetilde{X}}(x,p)=\sqrt{\eta}\,\bigl[p^{\top}[\nabla V(x)-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})]\bigr]_{+}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
            At each bounce, set
<math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}\leftarrow R_{\nabla V(x_{t})-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})}p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}\leftarrow R_{\nabla V(x_{t})-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})}p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Well-posedness and stationarity of the conditional phase space process)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every fixed <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y,\widetilde{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y,\widetilde{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and initial phase space point
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{0},p_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{0},p_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, the event-driven dynamics in <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, with
the reference point fixed at <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, defines a unique
non-explosive, time-homogeneous Markov process. The phase space measure
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\eta,y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\eta,y}</annotation></semantics></math> is stationary for this process. In particular, the
returned position <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined almost surely.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The well-posedness and stationarity assertions are proved in
<a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 The fixed-reference phase space process ‣ Appendix A Conditional half-turn process: construction, invariance, and implementation ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The induced Markov kernel on positions also satisfies the following
reversibility property.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Reversibility of the conditional half-turn kernel)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> be the Markov kernel on
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>; thus
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{y}(x,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{y}(x,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the law of the returned position <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>
for input <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is reversible with respect to
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is deferred to Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 The averaged ideal half-turn kernel ‣ Appendix A Conditional half-turn process: construction, invariance, and implementation ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition quantifies the stationary bounce count of the
ideal half-turn process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Stationary bounce count)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a universal constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\rm id}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\rm id}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that the
following holds. Suppose that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{0},Y)\sim\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{0},Y)\sim\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conditional on
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}\sim\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y}</annotation></semantics></math> be independent of
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> be independent of all position
variables. If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\rm b}^{\rm id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi><mi>id</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\rm b}^{\rm id}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of bounce events
in the resulting ideal half-turn process of <a href="#alg1" title="In 3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}N_{\rm b}^{\rm id}\leq K_{\rm id}\beta\eta\sqrt{d}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>b</mi><mi>id</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>id</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}N_{\rm b}^{\rm id}\leq K_{\rm id}\beta\eta\sqrt{d}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is deferred to
<a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Bounce complexity and capped-rate implementation ‣ Appendix A Conditional half-turn process: construction, invariance, and implementation ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></span>,
proof of <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Ideal Proximal BPS</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the conditional half-turn kernel in hand, we can now define the ideal
Proximal BPS. For a resampling probability <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, specified in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (𝜒^2 convergence). ‣ 3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, its
augmented-state transition kernel is</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{K}\bigl((x,y),\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\bigr)&amp;\coloneqq\delta_{2x-y}(\mathrm{d}y^{\prime})\,\bigl[\rho\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})+(1-\rho)\,\mathcal{H}_{y^{\prime}}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})\bigr]\,.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="1.200em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.300em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.300em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.300em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.022em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{K}\bigl((x,y),\mathrm{d}x^{\prime}\,\mathrm{d}y^{\prime}\bigr)&amp;\coloneqq\delta_{2x-y}(\mathrm{d}y^{\prime})\,\bigl[\rho\,\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}(\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})+(1-\rho)\,\mathcal{H}_{y^{\prime}}(x,\mathrm{d}x^{\prime})\bigr]\,.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The Dirac factor enforces <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}=2x-y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}=2x-y</annotation></semantics></math>; the two terms in brackets are
conditional resampling and the half-turn kernel. Thus
(<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 3.11 ‣ 3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>) is the formal version of the update
displayed in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Overview of the construction ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. The following algorithm
gives an equivalent simulation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Ideal Proximal BPS</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},Y_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},Y_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
