            <span id="alg2.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Invariance of the ideal Proximal BPS)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The transition kernel <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> in
(<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 3.11 ‣ 3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>) preserves the augmented law <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The reflection preserves <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 2.1 (Geometry of the joint law). ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.
At fixed <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the first term in brackets in
(<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 3.11 ‣ 3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>) preserves
<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> by definition, and the second does so by
<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 3.2 (Reversibility of the conditional half-turn kernel). ‣ 3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. The conditional mixture, and hence its
composition with the reflection, preserves <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The next theorem gives the quantitative convergence guarantee for
ideal Proximal BPS and also underlies the implementable guarantee
in <a href="#S4" title="4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Its proof is in
Appendices <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> and <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> convergence)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist universal constants
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{0},\rho_{\star}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{0},\rho_{\star}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that
the following holds. Assume (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1.1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>), and let
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\eta\leq c_{0}/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\eta\leq c_{0}/\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Set</span></p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\eta}\coloneqq\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\rho\coloneqq\rho_{\star}\sqrt{\alpha\eta\,\ell_{\eta}}\in(0,1/2]\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\eta}\coloneqq\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\rho\coloneqq\rho_{\star}\sqrt{\alpha\eta\,\ell_{\eta}}\in(0,1/2]\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be the transition kernel in
(<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 3.11 ‣ 3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.11</span></a>) with this choice of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for all integers <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</span></p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\geq\frac{C}{\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}\sqrt{\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\geq\frac{C}{\sqrt{\alpha\eta}}\sqrt{\log\frac{e}{\alpha\eta}}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all probability measures <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> on
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, and all integers <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\chi^{2}\bigl(\nu_{0}\mathcal{K}^{JN}\|\pi_{\eta}\bigr)\leq e^{-J}\chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})\,\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.022em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}\bigl(\nu_{0}\mathcal{K}^{JN}\|\pi_{\eta}\bigr)\leq e^{-J}\chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})\,\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently, the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>-marginal satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{TV}\bigl((\nu_{0}\mathcal{K}^{JN})^{X},\mu\bigr)\leq\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})}\exp\Bigl\{-\frac{J}{2}\Bigr\}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>TV</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>X</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>χ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>η</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>J</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{TV}\bigl((\nu_{0}\mathcal{K}^{JN})^{X},\mu\bigr)\leq\frac{1}{2}\sqrt{\chi^{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})}\exp\Bigl\{-\frac{J}{2}\Bigr\}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (𝜒^2 convergence). ‣ 3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> is a hypocoercive estimate. To see this,
let <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})</annotation></semantics></math> have mean zero and write</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}(y)\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[f(X,Y)\mid Y=y]\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\widetilde{f}(x,y)\coloneqq f(x,y)-\bar{f}(y)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}(y)\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[f(X,Y)\mid Y=y]\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\widetilde{f}(x,y)\coloneqq f(x,y)-\bar{f}(y)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Then, <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[\widetilde{f}(X,Y)\mid Y]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[\widetilde{f}(X,Y)\mid Y]=0</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f\|_{2}^{2}=\|\bar{f}\|_{2}^{2}+\|\widetilde{f}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f\|_{2}^{2}=\|\bar{f}\|_{2}^{2}+\|\widetilde{f}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the norm in <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}(\pi_{\eta})</annotation></semantics></math>. At fixed
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, conditional resampling removes <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the
conditional half-turn leaves <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}</annotation></semantics></math> unchanged and does not increase
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\widetilde{f}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\widetilde{f}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since reflection preserves the <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm,
the one-step Markov operator <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{K}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathsf{K}f\|_{2}^{2}-\|f\|_{2}^{2}\leq-\rho\,\|\widetilde{f}\|_{2}^{2}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝖪</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathsf{K}f\|_{2}^{2}-\|f\|_{2}^{2}\leq-\rho\,\|\widetilde{f}\|_{2}^{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The ordinary <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> estimate therefore gives no decay when
<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\bar{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\bar{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It remains to understand observables that depend only on <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. For
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=\bar{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=\bar{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, reflection changes <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}(2X-Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}(2X-Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be decomposed as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}(2X-Y)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[\bar{f}(2X-Y)\mid Y]+r_{f}(X,Y)\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[r_{f}(X,Y)\mid Y]=0\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.150em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}(2X-Y)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[\bar{f}(2X-Y)\mid Y]+r_{f}(X,Y)\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{\eta}}[r_{f}(X,Y)\mid Y]=0\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The first term still depends only on <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> averages to
zero when <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is resampled conditionally on <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is
centered, so is <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> shows
that the squared norm of the first term is at most
<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-c\alpha\eta)\,\|\bar{f}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-c\alpha\eta)\,\|\bar{f}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for a universal constant <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Reflection nevertheless preserves the total norm: the decrease
of the first component is balanced by the contribution from <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the usual <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm sees only a redistribution between the two
components, even though conditional resampling contracts the second one.
This interaction is the hypocoercive contraction
mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To capture this mechanism, we add a corrector to the ordinary
squared <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm and use the modified Lyapunov function</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\coloneqq\|f\|_{2}^{2}+\omega\mathscr{C}(f)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}_{\omega}(f)\coloneqq\|f\|_{2}^{2}+\omega\mathscr{C}(f)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">The corrector is chosen so that its one-step change contributes a negative
term proportional to <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\bar{f}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\bar{f}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with terms controlled by
<math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\widetilde{f}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\widetilde{f}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combined with the direct <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> estimate
above, this gives control of both components. The model-specific input
for the half-turn is <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition B.2 (Global half-turn bound). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>, which bounds its
contribution to the change of the corrector.
This is a discrete version of the modified-<math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> method of Dolbeault,
Mouhot, and Schmeiser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> (see also the sharpened formulation of Fan, Li, and
Lu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In the appendices, we denote by <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> the
conditional expectation given <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\coloneqq I-\mathsf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\coloneqq I-\mathsf{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let
<math id="S3.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{U}</annotation></semantics></math> denote pullback by the reflection <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\mapsto 2X-Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\mapsto 2X-Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}f=\bar{f}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptf_{\perp}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}f=\widetilde{f}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptr_{f}=\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{U}\mathsf{P}f\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>𝖯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝖯</mi><mo>⟂</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖴𝖯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathsf{P}}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}f=\bar{f}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptf_{\perp}\coloneqq\mathsf{P}^{\perp}f=\widetilde{f}\,,\hskip 20.00003ptr_{f}=\mathsf{P}^{\perp}\mathsf{U}\mathsf{P}f\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">The proof steps are organized as follows. Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Operator framework and micro–macro decomposition ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>
sets up the decomposition above and derives the ordinary <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> estimate.
Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a> states the two estimates
used to recover control of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathsf{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The reflection estimate is proved
in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Proof of ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>, and the half-turn estimate in
Appendices <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 The half-turn operator on affine functions ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>–<a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Proof of : From affine estimates to the half-turn bound ‣ Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>.
Finally, Appendix <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Modified 𝐿^2 contraction and proof of the main theorem ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a> constructs the modified energy, proves
its contraction, chooses <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> and the block length, and converts the
result into the stated <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and total variation bounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The use of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> analysis is partially motivated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, which gave obstructions to entropic convergence for piecewise-deterministic Markov processes such as the BPS.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section gives a gradient-only implementation of the ideal Proximal BPS
from <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Ideal Proximal BPS ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. It combines two ingredients. The
first is sampling from the conditional law
<math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}</annotation></semantics></math>. For this component, we import the first-order
rejection sampler and its RGO guarantee from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>; we specialize
that guarantee to the present conditional law, construct the required
approximate proximal point, and verify the prescribed first-order
residual bound. The second
ingredient is the conditional half-turn process, whose bounce events we
simulate with a capped rate. We then combine the imported RGO
approximation with the capped rate approximation of this process and bound
both the resulting total variation error and the number of gradient
queries.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Implementing the restricted Gaussian oracle</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We import both the first-order rejection algorithm and its analysis
from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. The theorem below records the specialization of their
RGO guarantee needed here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Restricted Gaussian oracle guarantee)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a universal constant <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\rm RGO}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>RGO</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\rm RGO}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that the
following holds.
Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose that
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{x}_{+}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{+}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|y-\eta\nabla V(\widehat{x}_{+})-\widehat{x}_{+}\|\leq\sqrt{d\eta}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|y-\eta\nabla V(\widehat{x}_{+})-\widehat{x}_{+}\|\leq\sqrt{d\eta}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the first-order rejection sampler of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Theorems 3.1 and
D.1]</cite> can be instantiated so that its output law <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\chi^{2}\bigl(\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}\bigm\|\widehat{\pi}\bigr)\leq\delta^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\chi^{2}\bigl(\widehat{\pi}\bigm\|\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}\bigr)\leq\delta^{2}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}\bigl(\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}\bigm\|\widehat{\pi}\bigr)\leq\delta^{2}\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\chi^{2}\bigl(\widehat{\pi}\bigm\|\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y=y}\bigr)\leq\delta^{2}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">using at most <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\rm RGO}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>RGO</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\rm RGO}</annotation></semantics></math> expected queries to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V</annotation></semantics></math>,
excluding the construction of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{x}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, provided that</span></p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{-1}\geq K_{\rm RGO}\beta\,\{\sqrt{d\log(1/\delta)}+\log(1/\delta)\}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>RGO</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{-1}\geq K_{\rm RGO}\beta\,\{\sqrt{d\log(1/\delta)}+\log(1/\delta)\}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The quantity in (<a href="#S4.E1" title="Equation 4.1 ‣ Theorem 4.1 (Restricted Gaussian oracle guarantee). ‣ 4.1 Implementing the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) is exactly
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\,\|\nabla V_{y}(\widehat{x}_{+})\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\,\|\nabla V_{y}(\widehat{x}_{+})\|</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{y}=V+\frac{\|\cdot-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{y}=V+\frac{\|\cdot-y\|^{2}}{2\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>. When
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\leq 1/\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\leq 1/\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, as in the application below,
(<a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 2.10 ‣ Item (i) ‣ Proposition 2.1 (Geometry of the joint law). ‣ 2.2 Proximal augmentation and the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>) shows that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha+\eta^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha+\eta^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>-strongly convex and
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta+\eta^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta+\eta^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth, with condition number at most two.
For example, gradient descent with step size
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2/(\alpha+\beta+2\eta^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2/(\alpha+\beta+2\eta^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> contracts the distance to the
minimizer by a universal factor at every iteration. The strong
convexity and smoothness bounds then convert this distance contraction
into the same geometric decrease, up to a factor of two, for the
quantity <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\,\|\nabla V_{y}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\,\|\nabla V_{y}\|</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, there is a universal
constant <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\rm prox}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>prox</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\rm prox}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that this method, initialized at
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, finds a point <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{x}_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{x}_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying
(<a href="#S4.E1" title="Equation 4.1 ‣ Theorem 4.1 (Restricted Gaussian oracle guarantee). ‣ 4.1 Implementing the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) using at most</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\rm prox}\,\Bigl[1+\log\Bigl(1+\frac{\|y-\eta\nabla V(x)-x\|}{\sqrt{d\eta}}\Bigr)\Bigr]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>prox</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\rm prox}\,\Bigl[1+\log\Bigl(1+\frac{\|y-\eta\nabla V(x)-x\|}{\sqrt{d\eta}}\Bigr)\Bigr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">gradient queries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. A gradient query certifies the
stopping condition, so neither function values nor an exact proximal
oracle are needed. We use this routine only through
(<a href="#S4.E4" title="Equation 4.4 ‣ 4.1 Implementing the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>); all of its queries, as well as those
of the rejection sampler, are included in <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\nabla V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\nabla V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Simulating the half-turn process</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For convenience, recall from (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 3.9In Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.2 The ideal conditional half-turn process ‣ 3 The Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a>) that the ideal
bounce rate along the half-turn process is</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{t}\coloneqq\lambda_{\widetilde{X}}(x_{t},p_{t})=\sqrt{\eta}\,\bigl[p_{t}^{\top}[\nabla V(x_{t})-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})]\bigr]_{+}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{t}\coloneqq\lambda_{\widetilde{X}}(x_{t},p_{t})=\sqrt{\eta}\,\bigl[p_{t}^{\top}[\nabla V(x_{t})-\nabla V(\widetilde{X})]\bigr]_{+}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This rate is not uniformly bounded along every path.
Given a deterministic cap
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\lambda}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\lambda}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we therefore replace it by the truncated rate
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\{\lambda_{t},\bar{\lambda}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\{\lambda_{t},\bar{\lambda}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and simulate the resulting process
by the homogeneous Poisson thinning construction reviewed in
<a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 The standard Bouncy Particle Sampler ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\pi}^{\rm id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>π</mi><mi>id</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\pi}^{\rm id}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\pi}^{\rm cap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>π</mi><mi>cap</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\pi}^{\rm cap}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the time-<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> position outputs of the ideal
and capped half-turn processes, respectively,
and let <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\nabla V}^{\rm cap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mi>cap</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\nabla V}^{\rm cap}</annotation></semantics></math> count the gradient queries used by
the capped simulation, assuming that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V(\widetilde{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V(\widetilde{X})</annotation></semantics></math> has
already been queried. For the ideal-versus-capped comparison below,
the two processes use the same reference point <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, drawn
exactly from <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\eta}^{X\mid Y}</annotation></semantics></math> independently of the current
position conditional on <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. This exact draw assumption only
isolates the capped rate error; <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Implementable Proximal BPS and total complexity ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> combines
the analysis with the approximate RGO of <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Implementing the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>
by coupling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both the ideal and capped processes evolve in continuous time without
time discretization; the latter differs only through its truncated
bounce rate.
For <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{y,\widetilde{x}}=y-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{y,\widetilde{x}}=y-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, the harmonic
flow over an interval of length <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is given exactly by</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}x_{t+u}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}}&amp;=(x_{t}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})\cos u+\sqrt{\eta}\,p_{t}\sin u\,,\\
p_{t+u}&amp;=-\eta^{-1/2}(x_{t}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})\sin u+p_{t}\cos u\,.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.300em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.300em">,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>η</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>cos</mi><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.300em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}x_{t+u}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}}&amp;=(x_{t}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})\cos u+\sqrt{\eta}\,p_{t}\sin u\,,\\
p_{t+u}&amp;=-\eta^{-1/2}(x_{t}-c_{y,\widetilde{x}})\sin u+p_{t}\cos u\,.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="alg1a" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Capped-rate simulation of the conditional half-turn process</figcaption>
<div id="alg1a.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l1a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> <math id="alg1.l1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, reference point <math id="alg1.l1a.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{x}</annotation></semantics></math>
with cached <math id="alg1.l1a.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V(\widetilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V(\widetilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math>, and rate cap
<math id="alg1.l1a.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Draw <math id="alg1.l2a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            Set <math id="alg1.l3a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\leftarrow x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\leftarrow x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l3a.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="alg1.l3a.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{y,\widetilde{x}}\leftarrow y-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{y,\widetilde{x}}\leftarrow y-\eta\nabla V(\widetilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l4a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <math id="alg1.l4a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;\pi</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4a.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              Draw a candidate waiting time
<math id="alg1.l5a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\sim\operatorname{Exp}(\bar{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\sim\operatorname{Exp}(\bar{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              Set <math id="alg1.l6a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{u}\leftarrow\min\{u,\pi-t\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{u}\leftarrow\min\{u,\pi-t\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              Propagate <math id="alg1.l7a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},p_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},p_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for time <math id="alg1.l7a.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{u}</annotation></semantics></math> using
(<a href="#S4.E5" title="Equation 4.5 ‣ 4.2 Simulating the half-turn process ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l8a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leftarrow t+\bar{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leftarrow t+\bar{u}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;\pi</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               Query <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla V(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla V(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and set
<math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{t}\leftarrow\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(x_{t},p_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{t}\leftarrow\lambda_{\widetilde{x}}(x_{t},p_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
               Draw <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\sim\operatorname{Unif}[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\sim\operatorname{Unif}[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\leq\min\{1,\lambda_{t}/\bar{\lambda}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\leq\min\{1,\lambda_{t}/\bar{\lambda}\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
                 <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}\leftarrow R_{\nabla V(x_{t})-\nabla V(\widetilde{x})}p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="1.280em">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}\leftarrow R_{\nabla V(x_{t})-\nabla V(\widetilde{x})}p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The cap <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is not required to dominate the ideal rate
<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <a href="#alg1a" title="In 4.2 Simulating the half-turn process ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> exactly simulates the
half-turn process with rate <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\{\lambda_{t},\bar{\lambda}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\{\lambda_{t},\bar{\lambda}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which serves as
an approximation to the ideal half-turn process. The next proposition bounds
the coupling error introduced by truncation and the gradient queries
used by the capped simulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Capped-rate approximation and query cost)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exist universal constants <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\rm evt}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>evt</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\rm evt}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\rm evt}\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>evt</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\rm evt}\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following holds whenever
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\eta\sqrt{d}\leq c_{\rm evt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>evt</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\eta\sqrt{d}\leq c_{\rm evt}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2.2em;">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\in(0,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\in(0,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and set</span></p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta}\coloneqq K_{\rm evt}\beta\eta\,\bigl(\sqrt{d\ell_{\xi,\Delta}}+\ell_{\xi,\Delta}\bigr)\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\text{where}\hskip 20.00003pt\ell_{\xi,\Delta}\coloneqq K_{\rm evt}\,\Bigl[\Delta+\log\frac{1}{\xi}\Bigr]\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>evt</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.150em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">where</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>evt</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ξ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta}\coloneqq K_{\rm evt}\beta\eta\,\bigl(\sqrt{d\ell_{\xi,\Delta}}+\ell_{\xi,\Delta}\bigr)\,,\hskip 20.00003pt\text{where}\hskip 20.00003pt\ell_{\xi,\Delta}\coloneqq K_{\rm evt}\,\Bigl[\Delta+\log\frac{1}{\xi}\Bigr]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every probability measure </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{0}\ll\pi_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{0}\ll\pi_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> satisfying
</span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})\leq\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}(\nu_{0}\|\pi_{\eta})\leq\Delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, consider the ideal half-turn process and
the capped half-turn process with rate cap
</span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\lambda}=\bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\lambda}=\bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, both started from
</span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},Y)\sim\nu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},Y)\sim\nu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. They can be coupled so that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{\nu_{0}}\bigl\{X_{\pi}^{\rm id}\neq X_{\pi}^{\rm cap}\bigr\}\leq\xi\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">{</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>π</mi><mi>id</mi></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>π</mi><mi>cap</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">}</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{\nu_{0}}\bigl\{X_{\pi}^{\rm id}\neq X_{\pi}^{\rm cap}\bigr\}\leq\xi\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For the same cap, every initial state, and every reference point,
the expected number of gradient queries used by the capped simulation
satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}N_{\nabla V}^{\rm cap}=\pi\bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta}=\pi K_{\rm evt}\beta\eta\,\bigl(\sqrt{d\ell_{\xi,\Delta}}+\ell_{\xi,\Delta}\bigr)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mi>cap</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>evt</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.150em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.150em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}N_{\nabla V}^{\rm cap}=\pi\bar{\lambda}_{\xi,\Delta}=\pi K_{\rm evt}\beta\eta\,\bigl(\sqrt{d\ell_{\xi,\Delta}}+\ell_{\xi,\Delta}\bigr)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The proof is deferred to
<a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Bounce complexity and capped-rate implementation ‣ Appendix A Conditional half-turn process: construction, invariance, and implementation ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref">Appendix <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></span>,
proof of <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Implementable Proximal BPS and total complexity</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#alg2a" title="In 4.3 Implementable Proximal BPS and total complexity ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> assembles the preceding components into
the complete gradient-only Proximal BPS algorithm. In each transition,
the approximate RGO produces an
approximate conditional draw <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is reused as in the ideal
construction: it becomes the next position on the resampling branch and
the reference point for the capped half-turn process in
<a href="#alg1a" title="In 4.2 Simulating the half-turn process ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Here,
<span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FirstOrderProx<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y,Q)</annotation></semantics></math></span> denotes a first-order solver for
<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, initialized at <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, that either returns a point satisfying
(<a href="#S4.E1" title="Equation 4.1 ‣ Theorem 4.1 (Restricted Gaussian oracle guarantee). ‣ 4.1 Implementing the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) within <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> gradient queries or
reports failure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A reported failure means only that the fixed query budget
was exhausted before the residual condition was certified; it can occur
because the initial residual in (<a href="#S4.E4" title="Equation 4.4 ‣ 4.1 Implementing the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>) is
unbounded over the state space. The sampler then returns its current
position, a fallback that preserves the
proximal solver’s query cap while requiring no additional queries.
The analysis treats the entire failure event as a coupling error, the fallback is not required to satisfy any accuracy constraints. The call
<span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ApproximateRGO<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y,\widehat{x}_{+},a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y,\widehat{x}_{+},a)</annotation></semantics></math></span> denotes the sampler in <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Restricted Gaussian oracle guarantee). ‣ 4.1 Implementing the restricted Gaussian oracle ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The parameter choices following <a href="#alg2a" title="In 4.3 Implementable Proximal BPS and total complexity ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>
jointly control the proximal solver, RGO, and capped-rate errors.
<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3 (Sampler from an order-2 Rényi warm start). ‣ Choice of parameters. ‣ 4.3 Implementable Proximal BPS and total complexity ‣ 4 Efficient implementation of Proximal BPS ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> then gives the resulting accuracy and expected
query complexity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2a" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Implementable Proximal BPS</figcaption>
<div id="alg2a.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l1a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> <math id="alg2.l1a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\sim\mu_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\sim\mu_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="alg2.l1a.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{2}(\mu_{0}\|\mu)\leq\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}(\mu_{0}\|\mu)\leq\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg2.l2a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameters:</span>
<math id="alg2.l2a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta,T,a,\rho,\bar{\lambda},Q_{\rm prox}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>prox</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta,T,a,\rho,\bar{\lambda},Q_{\rm prox}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3a.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            Draw <math id="alg2.l3a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> independently and set
<math id="alg2.l3a.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}\leftarrow X_{0}+\sqrt{\eta}Z_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>η</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}\leftarrow X_{0}+\sqrt{\eta}Z_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4a" class="ltx_listingline">
